      "text": "To find the \\(x\\)-intercept, we set \\(y = 0\\) in the equation of the parabola \\(x = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\\). This gives us:\n\n\\[\nx = 2(0)^2 - 6(0) + 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(x\\)-intercept is \\((3, 0)\\). Therefore, \\(a = 3\\).\n\nNext, to find the \\(y\\)-intercepts, we set \\(x = 0\\) in the equation of the parabola \\(x = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\\). This gives us a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\n0 = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 3\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 24}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(y\\)-intercepts are \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\). Therefore, \\(b = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(c = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b + c\\):\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3} + 3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 + \\frac{6}{2} = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b + c\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
