      "text": "To find the \\(x\\)-intercept of the parabola \\(x = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\\), we need to determine the value of \\(x\\) when \\(y = 0\\). Substituting \\(y = 0\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\nx = 2(0)^2 - 6(0) + 3 = 3\n\\]\nThus, the \\(x\\)-intercept is \\((3, 0)\\), so \\(a = 3\\).\n\nNext, to find the \\(y\\)-intercepts, we need to determine the values of \\(y\\) when \\(x = 0\\). Substituting \\(x = 0\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n0 = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\n\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in the standard form \\(2y^2 - 6y + 3 = 0\\). We solve this using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 3\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 24}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThus, the \\(y\\)-intercepts are \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\). So, \\(b = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(c = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nTo find \\(a + b + c\\), we add these values together:\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3} + 3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 + \\frac{6}{2} = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
